  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a convex optimization-based approach to minimize C\u2085. The strategy uses CVXPy with a two-phase method: \n- Phase 1: Initialize h using initial_h_values if available, otherwise a triangular waveform.\n- Phase 2: Formulate the problem as a convex optimization problem using a quadratic approximation to handle the non-linear objective.\nThis approach leverages CVXPy's efficient solvers while maintaining feasibility through linear constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u207a with h_i = h(x_i), x_i = 2i/n_points, i=0,...,n_points-1.\nMinimize: max\u2096 (h \u229b (1 - h))[k] * dx\nsubject to: \n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n- \u03a3 h_i = n_points / 2\n\nWe approximate the problem as a convex QP by relaxing the max with squared terms.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Load initial_h_values if provided; else, initialize h as triangular.\n2. Formulate QP in CVXPy:\n   - Variable h in [0,1]^n.\n   - Constraint \u03a3h = n_points/2.\n   - Objective: minimize squared l2 norm of correlations.\n3. Solve using CVXOPT solver with early stopping.\n4. Post-optimize: refine h via proximal gradient steps to minimize max overlap.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points=100.\n- Learning rate: 0.01 with decay.\n- QP iterations: 5000 with budget checks.\n- Proximal steps: 2000.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Solve QP in ~200s, leaving 400s for gradient refinement.\n- Monitor time, save best h every 60s.\n- Fall back to initial_h_values if no improvement.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Check h \u2208 [0,1], \u03a3h = n_points/2.\n- Compute exact C\u2085 via evaluator.\n- Return h, C\u2085, n_points.",
